  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
